so as we're now in October the month of  horror it seems like a perfect time to  return to the Cthulhu Mythos and we do  that today with the deep ones a set of  creatures that some of you may be  familiar with and others may just snow  as those weird fish people as of right  now most of what we've covered in this  channel regarding the works of Lovecraft  has centered around the deities of the  mythos the cosmic entities that we  cannot comprehend nor understand but the  deep ones are somewhat easier to explain  with that being said easier certainly  doesn't mean easy those of you who have  read the works of Lovecraft will know at  times it can be more than confusing so  what exactly are they the deep ones can  be broken down to and described as  amphibian like humanoids that dwell in  the very depths of the ocean  considering the very loose cannon of the  Cthulhu Mythos they don't seem to have a  set in stone appearance or form but they  are often described as having a mixture  of human fish and amphibians reigns  their skin is often somewhere between a  dark green and a light gray color their  bellies are white and they have this  noticeable Gloucester them suggesting  that a physical altercation may be quite  difficult and because of how slippery  their skin is the fish I suspect that  are deep ones is very apparent when we  look at their head because it is  basically the head of a fish we see an  array of gills located around the neck  and they have these large globular eyes  that in most cases don't have eyelids  making them incapable of blinking they  also have webbed hands and feet and  their backs are lined with fins and  scales despite the fact that they do  actually have legs their movement rarely  resembles that of a human the instead  move on all fours and at times hop  around like a frog a man-eating  carnivorous frog that is the deep ones  made their first appearance in love  cross 19:31 novella the shadow of  Innsmouth which was then later published  in 1936 until the state remains the only  piece of Lovecraft's work that was  published in a traditional book formed  during his lifetime the story itself  takes place in the fictional location of  Innsmouth  a deserted fish in town we very quickly  learned that those in the town have  quite a weird look to them and they're  definitely not the most welcoming to  strangers will several go in missing  including those sent by the government  to investigate the inner dealings of the  town when the narrator of the story and  our protagonists Robert Olmstead travels  to the town he comes across a local  merchant by the name of Obed Marsh  who discovered this race of fish like  humanoids Marcin took it upon him  of Crater copse known as the esoteric  order of Dagon this cult like many in  Lovecraft's work would are taken human  sacrifice and in exchange the deep ones  would ensure that their fishing holes  are very lucrative even gifting the  people of the town jewelry from the  depth of the ocean oh bird Marsh in his  cult would eventually be arrested and  the human sacrifice then stopped but the  deep ones angered then attacked the town  killing over half of its population the  only way the survivors could guarantee  their safety was by continuing Oh Birds  practices the people of Innsmouth were  then forced to breed with the deep ones  creating these rather odd hybrid  creatures that resembled humans early on  but as they age they slowly transformed  into deep ones they would eventually  leave the surface and live in the  undersea cities so it now makes sense  while the people of Innsmouth seem so  odd to the narrator why they looked and  walked so strangely because most of them  were going through the transformation of  becoming a deep one with an aura to  Robert now knowing too much it's no  longer safe in the town and as he tries  to escape the entire town begin their  search for him after a brief encounter  with some of the creatures Robert was  able to flee and as the years passed he  began to research his own family  bloodline - Robert Schoch he discovers  that he is a descendant of o bird Marsh  and this entire time he himself was  going through a transformation process  of becoming a deep one Robert eventually  comes to terms of his situation and  travels to the deep one city beneath the  sea where he would live for eternity so  now we've gone over the main crux of the  story there are a few things to discuss  and mention the deep ones are immortal  meaning the cannot die of old age and  decay but they can still however be  killed so if the deep ones are immortal  then why they breed in with humans we  may not know their final plan and  endgame but the deep ones aren't the  same as the cosmic entities subscribed  by Lovecraft  if we break them down they're  essentially an alien species a group of  intelligent frog people so we can  attempt to understand their motives and  designs we know that they want to take  over the surface so perhaps freedom of  humans is another way to increase their  numbers on the surface while  destabilizing our own population at the  same time it's also worth considering  that part of the cult sacrifice may not  have been a blood offering but maybe  just an offering of men and women to the  Deep Ones if you have another species  given you their people in exchange for  fish and jewelry then perhaps using them  to breed is the most useful way they can  be used there is some debate as to how  these hybrids are conceived with many  believing that all hybrids come from a  female deep on an  male human the theory behind this is  that female deep ones and male humans  possess the limiting growth gene of  their species which prevents the hybrid  upon some growing at a faster rates than  a regular deep ones which leaves us  wondering what type of monster could be  created from a female human and a male  deep one with all of that said we can  now discuss a transformation process of  a hybrid in some more detail in the  early life we've already mentioned that  they resembled a human and at this stage  they are not immortal so dying of  natural causes before the transformation  is complete is entirely possible later  in life their ears begin to shrink until  they are almost entirely non-existent in  some cases they just completely  disappear their eyes begin to bulge and  almost burst from their sockets no  longer needed to blink they begin to  lose all of their hair and their heads  begin to narrow women starts to form  between their fingers and their skin  becomes rubbery changing color gills are  developed from the folds and their neck  and their voice has become croaky the  actual rate in which one transforms  varies from person to person so there is  no set period for this transformation  process once they have transformed  however it takes some time before  they're able to live underwater so they  go into isolation until they are ready  they eventually abandon the human world  and journey to one of the many undersea  cities the deep ones may not be on the  same level as the deities of the Cthulhu  Mythos but they have been said to do  their bidding the Colton offer off story  was called the esoteric order of Dagon  Dagon himself is often described as an  enormous deep one and he was revered  amongst the deep ones as their deity  it's believed that all Deep Ones  continued to grow even once they've  reached maturity as long as they have a  steady diet so perhaps if enough time  passes the deep ones will be large  enough to take over the surface the  great dreamer Cthulhu was also amongst  the deities that the Deep Ones were said  to follow in the later stories they were  even said to be amongst a species that  protected his daughter Cthulhu so maybe  this association reflects their desire  to see the sunken city of early a once  again rise I know a lot of people have  been asking when I was going to return  to the Cthulhu Mythos and with it being  October most of 